1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,373
For as long as anyone alive can remember, Germany has had more people than Britain.

2
00:00:07,373 --> 00:00:09,600
Today, 84 million against 70.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:16,812
But on the United Nations' central projection, the UK passes Germany in 2059.

4
00:00:16,812 --> 00:00:23,400
Italy has already started shrinking. And Poland could lose half its people by 2100.

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:31,575
Here are Europe's biggest populations over 150 years, from 1950 to 2100.

6
00:00:33,175 --> 00:00:39,137
First, what you're looking at. Up to 2023, these are the United Nations' estimates.

7
00:00:39,137 --> 00:00:43,303
After that, they're projections: the UN's middle scenario,

8
00:00:43,303 --> 00:00:47,325
based on assumptions about births, deaths and migration.

9
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:50,429
Projections aren't predictions.

10
00:00:50,429 --> 00:00:53,855
They show where today's trends lead if they carry on.

11
00:00:53,855 --> 00:00:58,250
Whenever the chart is showing projected years, the bars are striped.

12
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:03,928
Russia is shown as the whole country, including the part in Asia.

13
00:01:03,928 --> 00:01:09,070
We've left out Turkey, which lies mostly in Asia. With 88 million people,

14
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:13,578
it would be second. And Germany is shown within today's borders,

15
00:01:13,578 --> 00:01:16,325
east and west together, for every year.

16
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:25,725
1950. Russia has 103 million people. Germany, 70 million. The UK, 50.

17
00:01:26,825 --> 00:01:35,150
Italy, 46. France, 42. Ukraine, 37. Spain has 28 million, and Poland 25.

18
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:41,259
Then comes the baby boom, and decades of growth almost everywhere.

19
00:01:41,259 --> 00:01:46,094
Russia peaks at the start of the nineties, at almost 150 million,

20
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:48,325
and Ukraine soon after, at 52.

21
00:01:52,325 --> 00:01:59,544
2000. Russia is already shrinking, at 147 million. Germany has 82.

22
00:01:59,544 --> 00:02:03,700
France and the UK, 59 each. Italy, 57.

23
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,475
Ukraine is down to 50 million.

24
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:10,941
Over the next quarter of a century,

25
00:02:10,941 --> 00:02:14,306
the countries in Western Europe keep growing slowly.

26
00:02:14,306 --> 00:02:17,025
Most of those further east keep shrinking.

27
00:02:21,025 --> 00:02:30,825
2025. Russia, 144 million. Germany, 84. The UK, just under 70. France, 67.

28
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:38,150
Italy, 59. Spain, 48. Ukraine, 39. And Poland, 38.

29
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:45,922
Here are six of them as lines. Look at Italy. Its population peaked in 2014,

30
00:02:45,922 --> 00:02:52,175
at 60.7 million. Since then, it has lost about one and a half million people.

31
00:02:53,275 --> 00:02:58,220
Spain, Poland and Germany are at, or very close to, their highest ever.

32
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:02,050
On the UN's numbers, all three start falling from here.

33
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:05,809
Now we're in projection territory.

34
00:03:05,809 --> 00:03:10,942
Everything from here on is the UN's middle scenario: where today's births,

35
00:03:10,942 --> 00:03:13,925
deaths and migration lead if they carry on.

36
00:03:16,925 --> 00:03:24,075
By 2050, Germany is down to 78 million, and the UK is up to 75 and a half.

37
00:03:25,175 --> 00:03:32,300
France has 68 million. Italy has lost more than 7 million people, and is down to 52.

38
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:40,750
And in 2059, the lines cross. With about 76 million each, the UK passes Germany.

39
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:46,782
By 2100, the UK has 74 million people, and Germany 71.

40
00:03:46,782 --> 00:03:52,526
The UK itself peaks in the early 2070s, then starts to shrink as well.

41
00:03:52,526 --> 00:03:58,681
France is the only one here still growing almost to the end of the century,

42
00:03:58,681 --> 00:04:01,800
to 68 and a half million in the 2090s.

43
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:09,218
Italy has fallen to 35 million. Spain, to 33. And Poland, to 19 million:

44
00:04:09,218 --> 00:04:11,500
half of what it has today.

45
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:18,981
Here's the full ranking for 2100. Russia is still first, at 126 million,

46
00:04:18,981 --> 00:04:24,535
almost 18 million fewer than today. Then the UK, Germany and France,

47
00:04:24,535 --> 00:04:26,250
and then a long drop.

48
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:33,045
Ukraine falls to 15 million, fewer than the Netherlands. Romania,

49
00:04:33,045 --> 00:04:37,425
which peaked in 1991 at 23 million, is down to 11.

50
00:04:39,025 --> 00:04:44,691
So why do the lines bend? Two forces decide most of it. The first is births.

51
00:04:44,691 --> 00:04:46,033
Without migration,

52
00:04:46,033 --> 00:04:51,625
a country needs about 2.1 children per woman to keep its population steady.

53
00:04:52,725 --> 00:05:01,034
No big European country is close. In 2023, France had the most, at 1.6. The UK,

54
00:05:01,034 --> 00:05:08,397
1.6 too. Germany, 1.4. Poland, 1.3. Italy and Spain, 1.2. And Ukraine,

55
00:05:08,397 --> 00:05:09,975
fewer than one.

56
00:05:11,375 --> 00:05:14,073
Fewer births means that in many countries,

57
00:05:14,073 --> 00:05:16,900
more people now die each year than are born.

58
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,692
In 2023, Germany had almost 4 more deaths than births for every 1,000 people. Italy,

59
00:05:24,692 --> 00:05:27,241
almost 5. Ukraine, 7 and a half.

60
00:05:27,241 --> 00:05:32,100
The UK and France still had slightly more births than deaths.

61
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:37,452
The second force is migration. And here's a surprise:

62
00:05:37,452 --> 00:05:40,435
Germany has more immigrants than the UK.

63
00:05:40,435 --> 00:05:46,550
One in five people living in Germany was born abroad. In the UK, about one in six.

64
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:52,564
Migration is what has kept Germany growing. But as its population ages,

65
00:05:52,564 --> 00:05:57,408
the gap between deaths and births gets wider, and on the UN's numbers,

66
00:05:57,408 --> 00:05:59,900
migration no longer makes up for it.

67
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,892
Spain has almost as high a share of immigrants as Germany,

68
00:06:04,892 --> 00:06:09,657
but one of Europe's lowest birth rates. Poland has very few immigrants,

69
00:06:09,657 --> 00:06:12,475
and many young Poles live and work abroad.

70
00:06:13,575 --> 00:06:20,115
Behind all of this is age. The median age is the age that splits a country in half:

71
00:06:20,115 --> 00:06:26,418
half the people are older, half younger. In 1950, it was 27 and a half in Italy.

72
00:06:26,418 --> 00:06:27,600
Today, it's 48.

73
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:32,426
Germany's median age is 45. The UK's is 40,

74
00:06:32,426 --> 00:06:37,019
which is one reason Britain keeps growing for longer.

75
00:06:37,019 --> 00:06:44,125
And Poland's has jumped from 25 to 42. On the UN's numbers, it reaches 52 by 2050.

76
00:06:45,725 --> 00:06:46,649
For Ukraine,

77
00:06:46,649 --> 00:06:52,577
the war since 2022 and millions of refugees have changed the numbers sharply.

78
00:06:52,577 --> 00:06:56,350
Its long-term projection is especially uncertain.

79
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:02,983
Three things to take away. One: on the UN's central projection,

80
00:07:02,983 --> 00:07:08,975
the UK passes Germany in 2059, because Germany has more deaths than births.

81
00:07:10,075 --> 00:07:15,079
Two: Italy, Spain and Poland are at or past their peak. By 2100,

82
00:07:15,079 --> 00:07:18,675
each could lose a third or more of its people.

83
00:07:19,775 --> 00:07:23,573
Three: a smaller population isn't a disaster by itself.

84
00:07:23,573 --> 00:07:30,825
But fewer young people supporting more older people is a challenge every country on this chart will face.

85
00:07:32,025 --> 00:07:39,650
Next on Terrachart: every European Cup winner, from 1956 to 2026, in one race.

86
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:46,472
Sources are in the description. Do you believe the 2100 projection for your country?

87
00:07:46,472 --> 00:07:49,550
What would change it? Tell us in the comments.
